  "summary_of_core_decisions": "The text argues that practical judgment can be a genuine source of motivation, not merely an instrument for satisfying antecedent desires. It defends a qualified rationalist position: motivation requires some engaged practical state, but that state can be generated by rational recognition rather than presupposed as an unmotivated desire. The soda-and-dime case shows that means-end reasoning transmits motivational significance without requiring a separate desire for each means, and the direction-of-fit distinction supports the possibility of rationally produced intentions.",
